From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bryan Hoban <bryan.hoban@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 13:28:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] kodi: drop work the Dolby Vision path does for nothing

Four things the native Dolby Vision path did on every video or every
frame without any use:

The adapter still carried the check of Kodi's GUI revision against the
pixels the engine found changed (CheckRevision, LogRevisions, the
ticket ring, DVPrivateOverlay::revisionSeen). It called
dvvk_overlay_changes, which the engine no longer exports, so it never
ran.

Two linear 3840x2160 GUI copy buffers (33 MB each) with their EGL
images, textures and framebuffers were made at every engine start.
Only the fallback that copies Kodi's GUI texture uses them; with the
GUI framebuffer read in place they are never touched. They are now
made when that fallback first needs one.

EndGuiComposite() cleared the EGL back buffer on every frame. In native
output that buffer is never presented: the window commits the engine's
buffers and does not swap, the GUI is drawn into its own framebuffer
and the video goes to the engine, so nothing draws into the back buffer
either. It is cleared once when native output begins, which keeps it
black for anything that reads it, and EndDVBridge() clears it itself
before the swap that ends the output. The flag is dropped at every swap
made under the tunnel.

WantsNativeOutput(stream) is asked by the player (twice), the codec
factory, the codec and the demuxer, the last one again at seeks. Every
call started the stream's telemetry over and logged the stream. The
function now remembers the stream it last announced, with the playback
it belonged to, and only a different one starts the telemetry and is
logged; the answer itself is computed as before on every call.
